.\" No version in the title line: see the note in irontile.1.
.TH START-IRONTILE 1 "" "irontile" "irontile"
.SH NAME
start\-irontile \- run irontile under a supervisor that restarts it if it crashes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B start\-irontile
.RB [ \-\-path
.IR PATH ]
.RI [ \-\- " ARGS..." ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B start\-irontile
is what the session entry runs, and what a login manager signals and waits on.
It starts
.BR irontile (1)
and starts it again if it crashes, so that a fault in the compositor costs the
windows rather than the whole session.
.PP
A clean exit ends the session, and so does being asked to stop: a login manager
ending the session and Ctrl+C at a terminal both arrive as a signal, which is
passed on to irontile and then honoured rather than treated as a crash.
Anything else is a crash, and irontile is started again.
.PP
With nothing after
.BR \-\- ,
irontile is started with
.BR \-\-session ,
because a supervised session is what this exists for.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-path " PATH"
The compositor to run. Default:
.BR irontile ,
found on
.BR PATH .
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show a usage message and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run the session with a configuration of your own:
.PP
.EX
start\-irontile \-\- \-\-config ~/irontile.toml
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/wayland\-sessions/irontile.desktop
The session entry. It names this program rather than the compositor, which is
the whole point of the two being separate.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR irontile (1),
.BR irontilectl (1)
